# =============================================================================
# MASES Phase 2 UI — Workspace build environment
# =============================================================================
#
# File:    mase_app/.env.workspace
# Phase:   1 (Frontend foundation) — Unit 1.4
# Version: 1.0.0
# Loaded:  When `vite build --mode workspace` is run (npm run
#          build:workspace or npm run build:all).
# Deploys: Built output (dist/) is served from
#          app.maalearna.com.et.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What this file is
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Vite's environment file for the workspace build. Vite loads the file
# whose suffix matches the --mode flag passed on the command line. The
# project's package.json uses --mode workspace for this entry, so this
# is the file that applies.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Vite's environment file precedence
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# When Vite starts a build or dev server, it loads environment files
# in this order. Later files override earlier ones:
#
#     1. .env                    (base — shared across every mode)
#     2. .env.local              (gitignored, developer overrides)
#     3. .env.workspace          (this file)
#     4. .env.workspace.local    (gitignored, per-dev workspace overrides)
#
# The two .local variants are not committed. Everything else is
# committed. There is no need to duplicate a value from .env into this
# file unless the workspace build needs to override it.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What belongs in this file
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Only PUBLIC configuration — values that are safe to ship inside the
# client bundle:
#
#   · API endpoints the browser will call.
#   · Public product names, feature flags, and version identifiers.
#   · Public third-party keys (analytics site IDs, public map keys).
#   · Build-time constants that affect the client's behavior.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What MUST NOT be placed in this file
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Anything secret. Every VITE_-prefixed value is inlined verbatim into
# the JavaScript bundle at build time and is visible to any user who
# opens DevTools. That includes:
#
#   · Backend secrets (APP_KEY, DB_PASSWORD, JWT signing keys).
#   · SMTP credentials.
#   · AI provider API keys (OpenAI, Anthropic).
#   · Private storage bucket credentials.
#   · Webhook signing secrets.
#
# Those live exclusively in the BACKEND .env file
# (api.maalearna.com.et/.env) and are never sent to the browser. If a
# value's presence in the frontend bundle would be a security incident,
# it does not belong here.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Variable conventions
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# · Every variable is prefixed with VITE_. Vite ignores any variable
#   without the prefix — a deliberate safety mechanism so that backend
#   values accidentally placed in a frontend env file are not inlined.
#
# · Values with spaces are quoted. Vite's dotenv parser strips
#   whitespace outside quotes; quoting preserves intentional spaces
#   and trailing characters.
#
# · No variable expansion is used. Values are complete literals.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TypeScript declaration
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Each variable in this file has a corresponding entry in
# src/vite-env.d.ts (Unit 1.4). That declaration is what makes
# import.meta.env.VITE_* type-check under vue-tsc. Adding a variable
# here without adding it there will produce a TS2339 ("Property does
# not exist") error during the type-check step of the build. Adding
# it to the declaration file without adding it here will produce a
# value of `undefined` at runtime, which is worse.
#
# The declaration file is the source of truth for what shape the
# environment is expected to have. This file is where the values are
# supplied.
# =============================================================================


# =============================================================================
# API base URL
# =============================================================================
#
# The origin of the MASES API, without a path suffix and without a
# trailing slash.
#
# Every request the client issues is composed by prepending this value
# to a path that starts with `/api/` or `/sanctum/`. See
# src/core/api/client.ts §"Purpose" for the composition:
#
#     VITE_API_BASE_URL + "/api/login"
#       = "https://api.maalearna.com.et/api/login"
#
# What this variable MUST contain:
#
#   · A scheme (`https://`). Not `http://` in production.
#   · A host (`api.maalearna.com.et`).
#   · Optionally a non-default port. (None today.)
#
# What this variable MUST NOT contain:
#
#   · A trailing slash. `https://api.maalearna.com.et/` would produce
#     `https://api.maalearna.com.et//api/login` — the doubled slash is
#     usually harmless on the server side but breaks any CORS origin
#     comparison that inspects the exact request URL.
#   · A path segment. `https://api.maalearna.com.et/api` would
#     produce `https://api.maalearna.com.et/api/api/login`.
#
# If this value is missing, the build fails at `vue-tsc --noEmit`
# (the variable is declared non-optional in vite-env.d.ts, Unit 1.4),
# before Vite even runs. This is intentional — a production build that
# silently lacks an API URL would produce a broken deploy.
#
VITE_API_BASE_URL="https://api.maalearna.com.et"


# =============================================================================
# Application name
# =============================================================================
#
# The product's display name. Consumed in two places:
#
#   1. src/features/auth/AuthLayout/AuthLayout.vue — as the default
#      `alt` text for the logo image, when the consumer does not
#      supply an explicit `logoAlt` prop.
#
#   2. A future App.vue (Unit 12.13) — as the fallback value for
#      document.title, when a route does not supply its own title.
#      The auth pages do not have their own titles today, so the
#      value reads as the browser tab caption before the router
#      mounts a page.
#
# This value differs between the two builds:
#
#   · Workspace (this file)  → "MASES"           — internal tool.
#   · Portal (.env.portal)   → "Maale Arna Portal" — shareholder
#                                                  surface.
#
# The distinction is deliberate. See portal-entry/index.html
# §"Why <title>Maale Arna Portal</title>" for the reasoning about the
# two brand voices.
#
# If this value is missing, AuthLayout falls back to a generic string
# ("Application logo"). The build does not fail; the label is merely
# less useful to a screen reader. Adding it is recommended.
#
VITE_APP_NAME="ማሴስ ወርክስፔስ"


# =============================================================================
# Reserved for future use (do not uncomment without a code change)
# =============================================================================
#
# The variables below are not read by any current code. They are
# documented here as a namespace reservation, so that a future change
# that needs one knows the name has already been chosen and does not
# invent a competing variant. Uncommenting a line without adding the
# corresponding read in the code produces a dead value and a
# support-ticket risk (someone changes the .env expecting a behavior
# change that does not occur).
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Build identifier — useful for support correlation
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# VITE_APP_VERSION="0.1.0"
#
# When set, this value would be embedded in a build-info footer or a
# console banner, so that support can identify the deployed version
# from a user's screenshot. Would be read by src/core/bootstrap.ts or
# a dedicated BuildInfo component.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Environment label — distinguishes staging from production
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# VITE_APP_ENV="production"
#
# When set, this value would gate optional dev-only tooling (a debug
# overlay, a performance HUD) that should never appear in production
# builds. Currently src/core/api/client.ts uses import.meta.env.DEV,
# which Vite sets to false in any production build. VITE_APP_ENV
# would be a second, coarser signal — useful if a staging deploy is
# built with the production flag but should still expose diagnostics.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Feature flags
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# VITE_FLAG_PHONE_LOGIN="true"
#
# Feature flags are a separate architecture decision. The freeze has
# not allocated a flag namespace yet. If one is introduced, the
# convention is likely VITE_FLAG_<NAME> with values "true" or "false"
# (as strings — Vite does not parse booleans). Do not add flags
# ad-hoc; each one should be reviewed as part of the feature it gates.
#
# =============================================================================